  "notes": "AlPO$_{4}$-53(A)",
  "comments": "(Kirchner, 2000) found the \\href{current_web_home_dir/Tutorials/SiO2_AlPO4}{aluminum phospate zeolite} AlPO$_{4}$-53 in three states: \\begin{itemize} \\item \\href{current_web_home_dir/A3BCD14E3_oP176_19_6a_2a_2a_28a_6a-001}{As-synthesized AlPO$_{4}$-53(A) [24(AlPO$_{4}$)$\\cdot$8.5CH$_{3}$NH$_{2}$$\\cdot$14H$_{2}$O]} (this structure) includes methyl and ammonium ions as well as water. \\item \\href{current_web_home_dir/AB4C_oP144_61_3c_12c_3c-001}{Orthorhombic AlPO$_{4}$-53(B)} is calcined (heated below the melting temperature) to remove those ions and molecules, leaving the framework structure. \\item \\href{current_web_home_dir/AB4C_mC72_5_3c_12c_3c-001}{Monoclinic AlPO$_{4}$-53(C)} is produced by heating AlPO$_{4}$-53(B) above 700$^\\circ$C. \\end{itemize} ~ \\vspace{-.25in} \n (Kirchner, 2000) did not give the positions of any hydrogen atoms.  The final two oxygen atoms, O XXVII and O XXVIII, represent the positions of the water molecules. \n Space group $P2_{1}2_{1}2_{1}$ \\#19 is \\href{current_web_home_dir/Tutorials/ChiralSpaceGroups#ClassIII}{chiral, Sohncke Class III}.  Any mirror operation on the coordinates, {\\em e.g.}, $(x y z) \\rightarrow (-x -y -z)$, will produce a mirror image of the current structure, still in space group $P2_{1}2_{1}2_{1}$, with the same interatomic distances and bond angles."
